1
00:00:00,145 --> 00:00:03,250
Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:05,406
If you're new here, welcome.

3
00:00:05,406 --> 00:00:09,239
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

4
00:00:09,838 --> 00:00:15,389
A town hall that stood for about a hundred and forty years, one that came through

5
00:00:15,389 --> 00:00:19,248
two world wars, burned down in a single evening.

6
00:00:19,248 --> 00:00:24,788
That happened in Limoges, in France, and it is only one part of the worst unrest the

7
00:00:24,788 --> 00:00:25,870
country has seen in years.

8
00:00:25,870 --> 00:00:28,627
So what is going on in France, and where could it go next?

9
00:00:29,371 --> 00:00:35,244
I'm Mittal Shah, or rather an AI-generated version of my voice, and I'll be taking

10
00:00:35,244 --> 00:00:36,676
you through today's story.

11
00:00:37,545 --> 00:00:38,354
First, what happened.

12
00:00:38,354 --> 00:00:42,815
It began last month with high-school students in the northern suburbs of Paris.

13
00:00:42,815 --> 00:00:46,760
They were angry about teacher shortages and run-down school buildings.

14
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:52,525
Over time, the anger widened to the cost of living and the state of public services.

15
00:00:52,525 --> 00:00:58,056
Then, on Saturday the seventeenth of October, it turned violent.

16
00:00:58,056 --> 00:01:02,927
The interior ministry says about five lakh and fifty thousand people joined protests

17
00:01:02,927 --> 00:01:05,829
across the country, around a lakh of them in Paris.

18
00:01:06,454 --> 00:01:12,936
In Limoges, a crowd broke into the town hall in the evening and set it on fire.

19
00:01:12,936 --> 00:01:15,944
Its spire collapsed, and the building was largely destroyed.

20
00:01:15,944 --> 00:01:18,248
It had been opened in eighteen eighty-three.

21
00:01:18,248 --> 00:01:24,347
In Marseille, protesters broke into the city hall and covered it in graffiti.

22
00:01:24,347 --> 00:01:26,117
In Lyon, the tourist office was set on fire.

23
00:01:26,117 --> 00:01:31,219
Shops were looted in several cities, and more than three thousand were hit.

24
00:01:31,857 --> 00:01:33,605
Now, why is this happening?

25
00:01:33,605 --> 00:01:34,710
Money.

26
00:01:34,710 --> 00:01:38,982
France spends more than it earns, and that gap is called the budget deficit.

27
00:01:38,982 --> 00:01:44,445
This year it is about five point four per cent of the country's economic output, one

28
00:01:44,445 --> 00:01:46,693
of the highest in the euro zone.

29
00:01:46,693 --> 00:01:49,189
The government wants it down to five per cent next year.

30
00:01:49,189 --> 00:01:55,381
Its total debt is about a hundred and nineteen per cent of a year's output, a

31
00:01:55,381 --> 00:01:56,491
post-war record.

32
00:01:57,126 --> 00:02:01,619
To cut the deficit, the government has to pass a budget with spending cuts, and that

33
00:02:01,619 --> 00:02:04,204
is exactly what people are angry about.

34
00:02:04,204 --> 00:02:07,426
It also leads a minority government.

35
00:02:07,426 --> 00:02:12,468
That means it does not have enough votes in parliament on its own, and it can be

36
00:02:12,468 --> 00:02:15,181
toppled if it cannot find a compromise.

37
00:02:15,775 --> 00:02:18,190
Here is where the markets come in.

38
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:22,827
A government bond is an I-O-U, which simply means, I owe you.

39
00:02:22,827 --> 00:02:27,068
Investors lend money to the government, and the government promises to pay it back

40
00:02:27,068 --> 00:02:27,884
with interest.

41
00:02:27,884 --> 00:02:32,695
When investors feel it is riskier to lend, they ask for more interest, and that rate

42
00:02:32,695 --> 00:02:34,296
is called the yield.

43
00:02:34,296 --> 00:02:39,147
France's ten-year yield is now close to five per cent, the highest in about

44
00:02:39,147 --> 00:02:40,103
twenty-four years.

45
00:02:40,103 --> 00:02:43,836
A higher yield means France pays more to borrow.

46
00:02:44,495 --> 00:02:46,578
So how is the government responding?

47
00:02:46,578 --> 00:02:51,496
The Prime Minister has promised to send in troops to work alongside the police, with

48
00:02:51,496 --> 00:02:54,216
a massive deployment planned for this Saturday.

49
00:02:54,216 --> 00:02:57,224
He has also admitted failures:

50
00:02:57,224 --> 00:03:03,726
poor intelligence, police stretched too thin in fixed positions, and planned police

51
00:03:03,726 --> 00:03:04,889
leave that was not cancelled.

52
00:03:04,889 --> 00:03:06,635
The interior minister apologised.

53
00:03:06,635 --> 00:03:10,941
Curfews for minors are in place in Marseille and Lille.

54
00:03:10,941 --> 00:03:17,604
Parliament will set up a commission of inquiry, about two hundred investigators are

55
00:03:17,604 --> 00:03:22,910
tracking those involved, and a man suspected of starting the Limoges fire has been

56
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:23,565
arrested.

57
00:03:23,565 --> 00:03:26,438
The government has blamed far-left groups.

58
00:03:26,438 --> 00:03:30,654
Those groups say the real problem is that living costs and public services were

59
00:03:30,654 --> 00:03:31,256
ignored.

60
00:03:31,731 --> 00:03:32,975
Will it spread to other countries?

61
00:03:32,975 --> 00:03:37,163
Not so far in the same form, but there are signs.

62
00:03:37,163 --> 00:03:42,681
Italy has seen riots in Milan and other northern cities, and its defence minister

63
00:03:42,681 --> 00:03:46,497
called them hybrid warfare organised through social media.

64
00:03:46,497 --> 00:03:49,033
That is his view, not an established fact.

65
00:03:49,033 --> 00:03:52,904
Whether this becomes a wider movement, nobody can say yet.

66
00:03:53,437 --> 00:03:55,136
The bigger question is politics.

67
00:03:55,136 --> 00:04:00,456
France holds a presidential election next year, and candidates on the far right and

68
00:04:00,456 --> 00:04:02,455
the hard left are polling strongly.

69
00:04:02,455 --> 00:04:08,299
If the government falls over the budget, early parliamentary elections are possible.

70
00:04:08,299 --> 00:04:13,698
Unions have called strikes for the fifth of November, and more protests are planned

71
00:04:13,698 --> 00:04:18,431
for Saturday, from student unions and from the Yellow Vest groups angry about fuel

72
00:04:18,431 --> 00:04:19,031
prices.

73
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:20,800
And what about India?

74
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:23,975
There is no direct effect we can point to today.

75
00:04:23,975 --> 00:04:28,964
One thing worth watching is the trade deal between India and the European Union.

76
00:04:28,964 --> 00:04:34,556
It is meant to be signed in December, and a distracted French government could make

77
00:04:34,556 --> 00:04:37,197
European politics harder to predict.

78
00:04:37,197 --> 00:04:39,671
But that is only a possibility.

79
00:04:40,331 --> 00:04:41,225
To sum up:

80
00:04:41,225 --> 00:04:46,388
a school protest has grown into a test of whether France can fix its budget and keep

81
00:04:46,388 --> 00:04:49,437
the peace, and the next few weeks will show which way it goes.

82
00:04:50,333 --> 00:04:53,244
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode.

